claim("In that model there is a threshold supply flow below which the reed does not sound (silent at 1.0 m/s, sounding at 1.5 m/s supply velocity through 30 mm2), the sounding frequency lies below the reed's natural frequency (440.7 Hz against 444 Hz, -13 cents) and falls slightly as the supply rises (-13.0 to -13.8 cents from 1.5 to 3.5 m/s), the tip amplitude grows with supply (1.3 to 2.4 mm), and at a much larger fixed supply flow (4 m/s) the reed is pushed through the plate and stops.",
      "numerical", True, [("s03", "runs list"), ("millot", "section 3.2, figures 10 to 17 (qualitative agreement)")], "verified", ["D-011", "T-101", "T-102", "T-103"], "partly-supported", "The input file says pitch 'drops flat under excessive airflow'; the model shows a small fall and then choking. Measured cents-per-pascal figures for real reeds were not found.", FR)
claim("In that model the reed tip motion stays close to sinusoidal (second harmonic of the displacement between -53 and -43 dB) while the volume flow through the reed carries strong harmonics that grow with the supply (second harmonic of the flow from -18.5 dB to -8.9 dB re the fundamental), so the harmonic content of the sound comes from the flow law, not from the reed motion.",
      "numerical", True, [("s03", "flow_harmonics_dB, reed_h2_dB"), ("millot", "section 2.1 first paragraph; section 3.3")], "verified", ["D-011", "T-104"], "supported", "", FR)
claim("A (-,+) reed in the minimal configuration oscillates only when V1*L2*w^2/(c0^2*S2) exceeds 1 (the upstream volume and inertance must be large enough); a (+,-) reed needs the opposite inequality.",
      "numerical", True, [("millot", "section 3.1, instability condition after eq. (10)"), ("s03b", "L1 = 60, 30 mm oscillate (ratio 2.54, 1.27); L1 = 20, 10 mm do not (0.85, 0.42)")], "verified", ["D-011", "D-012", "T-105"], "partly-supported", "The input file's 'upstream inertial effect' is right in kind; its statements about the ratio of reed frequency to Helmholtz frequency were not traced.", FR)
claim("In Fletcher's sign convention as used by Millot and Baumann, (-,+) denotes a reed blown closed and (+,-) a reed blown open; a Western free reed in normal use is the (-,+) kind, and the same reed can act as a (+,-) reed when air flows the other way (as the second reed of a harmonica channel does). 'Inward-striking' is not a term the free-reed source uses.",
      "bibliographic", False, [("millot", "abstract; section 2.1; Figure 1"), ("r1-A-free-reed.json", "claims A6, A7")], "single-source", ["D-010"], "partly-supported", "", FR)
claim("The reed passes through its slot during a noticeable part of each period, so the escape area opens on both sides of the plate (two flow pulses per cycle).",
      "software", False, [("millot", "section 3.3, discussion of Figure 17"), ("s03", "goes_through_plate true for every sounding run")], "verified", ["D-011"], "supported", "Verified for the model; a measurement on a real reed was not read.", FR)
claim("The first clamped-free bending frequency of a uniform rectangular reed is f1 = (1.875104^2/(2 pi sqrt(12))) (t/L^2) sqrt(E/rho) = 0.16154 (t/L^2) sqrt(E/rho), and the input file's reed-length table follows from it to within 0.005 mm.",
      "mathematical", False, [("s01", "reed_table_max_abs_diff_mm"), ("r1-A-free-reed.json", "claims A1, A2")], "verified", [], "supported", "Arithmetic only. The plan does not model reed geometry: each reed is specified by its natural frequency, Q, effective areas and offsets.", FR)
claim("The input file's statements that threshold pressure was compared for reeds of Q 16, 19, 29, 55 and about 250, that a closing reed needs more than 2 kPa when its frequency is below the cavity's Helmholtz frequency, and that laser vibrometry shows sinusoidal motion at 0.2 kPa and strong harmonics at 1.4 to 1.6 kPa, could not be traced to any source.",
      "bibliographic", False, [("r1-A-free-reed.json", "claims A11, A13"), ("in", "lines 18 and 21")], "inferred", [], "not-found", "Not used by the plan.", FR)
claim("Ricot, Causse and Misdariis (J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 117(4), 2279, 2005) exists and, by its abstract, treats the accordion reed as a blown-closed free reed without acoustic coupling and attributes the radiated sound mainly to a dipole source; St. Hilaire, Wilson and Beavers (J. Fluid Mech. 49, 803, 1971) and Tarnopolsky, Fletcher and Lai (J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 108, 400, 2000) exist.",
      "bibliographic", False, [("ricot", "abstract, through search results only"), ("r1-A-free-reed.json", "claims A9, A10"), ("millot", "reference list")], "single-source", ["D-013"], "partly-supported", "Full texts not read (A-012, publisher refusals). The plan's radiation stage is a design choice (D-013) checked by listening, not a consequence of this claim.", FR)
claim("Published synthesis work on free reeds that an implementer could consult exists: a physics-based harmonium reed synthesis (DAFx 2023), a time-domain reed and airflow scheme compared with laser vibrometry (Forum Acusticum 2023), and a thesis applying the Millot-Baumann model to the harmonica.",
      "bibliographic", False, [("r1-B-harmonica.json", "claims B6, B24"), ("r1-C-accordion-melodica.json", "claim C17")], "single-source", [], "not-in-input", "Seen in search results only.", FR)
