claim("The partials of a stiff string are f_n = n*f0*sqrt(1 + B*n^2) with B = pi^3*E*d^4/(64*T*L^2) for a solid round wire, equivalently B = pi^2*E*d^2/(64*rho*L^4*f0^2).",
      "mathematical", True, [("woodhouse", "eq. (4): the bending term E*pi^2*d^2*n^2/(64*rho*L^4*f1^2) is B*n^2"), ("jos_stiff", "stiff-string wave equation and dispersion"), ("r1-D-plucked-string.json", "claims D1, D2 (Fletcher and Rossing form)"), ("s01", "strings table reproduced")], "corroborated", ["D-060", "T-130"], "supported", "", ST)
claim("The loss factor of string mode n can be modelled as the sum of a bridge term eta_b = 2*Re(Y)/(pi*n*Y0) with Y0 = 1/sqrt(T*m), an air term eta_air = (rho_a/rho)*(2*sqrt(2)*M + 1)/M^2 with M = (d/4)*sqrt(2*pi*f_n/nu_a), rho_a = 1.2 kg/m3, nu_a = 1.5e-5 m2/s, and a bending term eta_bend = E*pi^2*d^2*n^2*eta_E/(64*rho*L^4*f1^2 + E*pi^2*d^2*n^2); air damping dominates low modes and bending damping high modes.",
      "mathematical", True, [("woodhouse", "section II A, eqs (1)-(4)")], "single-source", ["D-061", "T-131"], "not-in-input", "One Tier 1 source, read in full; its air term is quoted from Fletcher and Rossing (1998). The tests check the implementation against these formulas and check qualitative ordering; calibration to recordings is a later step.", ST)
claim("Two strings of a course that share a bridge form a symmetric mode that drives the bridge and decays fast and an antisymmetric mode that hardly drives it and decays slowly; with a slight mistuning a note plucked on one or both strings shows a fast first decay followed by a slow second decay; with weak coupling and mistuning the envelope beats at the difference frequency; with strong resistive coupling the two frequencies lock and the beat disappears.",
      "numerical", True, [("s04", "unison_both, mistuned_1.0c, detuned_2c_weak, detuned_8c_weak, 3c_strong_coupling_mode_split_Hz"), ("woodhouse", "sections I and III"), ("jos_coupled", "two-stage decay paragraph")], "verified", ["D-062", "T-132", "T-133"], "supported", "With an exact unison and equal plucks the antisymmetric mode is not excited and no aftersound appears in a one-polarisation model; the model therefore keeps a small default mistuning.", ST)
claim("For the bowl-back mandolin Woodhouse measured (a 'Stridente' Neapolitan instrument with La Bella 770L strings), double decays are predicted for most notes above about 500 Hz and for none in the lowest octave; his coupled-pair example varies the mistuning over plus or minus 8 cents.",
      "bibliographic", False, [("woodhouse", "section II B, Figure 4 text; section III")], "single-source", ["D-062"], "supported", "One instrument. The plan does not assert a 500 Hz boundary; the behaviour follows from the bridge admittance preset.", MA)
claim("A string plucked at a fraction beta of its length has partial n excited in proportion to sin(n*pi*beta)/n^2 in displacement (so partials at multiples of 1/beta vanish), and a plectrum of finite width and compliance adds a low-pass effect.",
      "mathematical", True, [("r1-D-plucked-string.json", "claim D19 (CCRMA text and DAFx paper)"), ("jos_stiff", "context")], "single-source", ["D-063", "T-134"], "supported", "Textbook result; the Tier 1 text was read by the round-1 worker, not re-read by the planner.", ST)
claim("Modal synthesis (a bank of exponentially decaying sinusoidal modes whose complex frequencies come from the coupled string, bridge and body system) reproduces the coupled-pair behaviour exactly for a linear model, because the response is the sum of the eigenmodes' decaying exponentials.",
      "software", True, [("s04", "envelope computed from the eigen-decomposition"), ("K-20261009T120109Z-two-pole-resonator-with-input-zero-gives-cosine-start", "resonator form")], "verified", ["D-060", "D-062"], "not-in-input", "", ST)
claim("Weinreich's 'Coupled piano strings' (J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 62, 1474, 1977), Perng's Stanford thesis on the harpsichord plectrum (2012), and Rauhala and Valimaki's tunable dispersion filter design (2006) exist as the input file or common practice cite them; the input file's '2 x 2 complex bridge admittance matrix' wording was not found in a source that could be opened.",
      "bibliographic", False, [("r1-D-plucked-string.json", "claims D6, D16, D17, D21")], "single-source", [], "partly-supported", "", ST)
claim("The Helmholtz frequency of a cavity with a hole is f = (c/(2*pi))*sqrt(S/(V*L_eff)) with L_eff the hole thickness plus end corrections (about 0.85 r per flanged end, 0.61 r per unflanged end).",
      "mathematical", False, [("w_helm", "resonant frequency formula"), ("r1-D-plucked-string.json", "claims D23, D24")], "single-source", [], "supported", "Not used: body modes are preset data, not computed from geometry.", ST)
